00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.100
Okay, let's pop the hood on deferred

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:05.400
positional encoding in KV caches.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.900
Everyone treats positional encoding as a fixed property

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.360
baked into the KV cache at right time.

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:14.140
In practice, it is a separate transformation

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.980
that can be applied later inside the attention kernel.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:20.220
Lazy attention keeps raw keys and values

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.040
without positional offsets, then injects rotary

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:26.580
or ALIBI adjustments on the fly during the mat mall.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:30.320
The approach removes the need to materialize new caches

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:33.300
when the same prefix is reused at different positions.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:37.920
It cuts time to first token by roughly 1.37 times

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.900
under skewed document distributions.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.800
The tradeoff appears in kernel complexity

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:46.660
because every attention call now carries extra index arithmetic.

00:06:47.500 --> 00:06:51.400
That overhead stays under 5% on modern GPUs.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:55.880
Teams running long context rag should adopt deferred encoding

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:59.300
when prefix reuse exceeds 30% of total tokens.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:03.660
Otherwise, the simpler static cache remains easier to debug.

